WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280
In two parts.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:54.480
There are some four million different kinds of animals and plants in the world.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.000
Four million different solutions to the problems of staying alive.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:06.000
This is the story of how a few of them came to be as they are.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:52.000
The South American rainforest, the richest and most varied assemblage of life in the world.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.000
There are around 50 different kinds of monkeys in these forests.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Some of the most beautiful creatures here are hummingbirds.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.000
54 different kinds have been found within a few miles of where I'm standing.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
And over 300 have been found in South America as a whole.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.000
In fact, nobody knows exactly how many different kinds of animals there are here.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Wherever you look, there's life.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:37.000
There are several hundred thousand different insects that have been named,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.000
and without doubt many hundreds more that haven't.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:51.000
And all these varied creatures and plants form one complex mosaic.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
The orchid needs the bee to pollinate it.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.000
The anteater couldn't have existed before the ants,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.000
so unless the whole complex was brought into existence in one flash of instant creation,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.000
different organisms must have appeared at different times.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.000
But which came first, and why should there be such an immense variety?

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:22.000
Such questions obsessed a young 24-year-old Englishman who came to these forests in 1832.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.000
His name was Charles Darwin, and he was enthralled almost to the point of ecstasy

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.000
by the richness of life that he found here.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:39.000
One day, in a small area, he discovered 69 different species of beetle.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.000
As he wrote in his journal, it's enough to disturb the composure of the entomologist's mind

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:51.000
to contemplate the future dimension of a complete catalogue.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.000
The conventional view of the time was that each and every species of animal and plant

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:07.000
had been individually created by God, and Darwin was no atheist.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
During the next three years, the beagle sailed around South America and up into the Pacific.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:41.000
600 miles west of Ecuador, they came to the lonely Galapagos Islands.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.000
It was here on these volcanic islands that Darwin's doubts and puzzles

00:04:45.000 --> 00:05:00.000
about the creation of species were reawakened.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.000
Everywhere, Darwin found creatures that bore a general resemblance

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:10.000
to those that he had seen on the mainland.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.000
But nearly all were slightly different.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.000
These, for example, were, without doubt, cormorants,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.000
similar to those that he had seen flying along Brazilian rivers.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.000
But here in the Galapagos, their wings were so small and with such stunted feathers

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.000
that the birds had lost their powers of flight.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.000
And these were clearly iguanas.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.000
He had seen such creatures climbing trees and eating leaves in the South American forests.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.000
But here on the Galapagos, where there was little vegetation,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.000
these iguanas fed on seaweed, and they were not the same.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.000
Smaller, darker, and with unusually long claws

00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:02.000
to help them keep a foothold among the crashing breakers.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.000
They also had extraordinary habits.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:24.000
Swimming fearlessly out to sea and diving deep to graze on the seabed.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.000
The Galapagos Islands got their name from the herds of tortoises that live on them,

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.000
which sailors for centuries had slaughtered for food.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.000
But they too were very obviously different from any mainland tortoise.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.000
They were many, many times bigger.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:46.000
The English vice-governor of the islands told Darwin that he could tell without difficulty

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:53.000
which island a particular tortoise came from by its shape.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:57.000
This one, for example, with its deep, rounded shell,

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:06.000
is a watered island where it can feed mainly on vegetation on the ground.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.000
This one, on the other hand, has a peak to the front of its shell

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.000
that enables it to stretch its long neck upwards.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:17.000
It comes from an arid island where the tortoises often have to crane up

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.000
to reach the only food available, the branches of trees and cactus.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.000
The suspicion grew in Darwin's mind that species were not fixed forever.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.000
Perhaps these tortoises were all descended from common ancestors

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.000
and had changed to suit their particular islands.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:41.000
The differences that Darwin had noticed amongst these Galapagos animals

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.000
were, of course, all tiny.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:47.000
But if they could develop, wasn't it possible that over the thousands

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:51.000
or millions of years a whole series of such differences

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.000
might add up to one revolutionary change?

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.000
Was it not possible, perhaps, that in the past amphibians had developed

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.000
watertight skins and so turned into reptiles?

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Or that a lizard-like reptile had developed a feathery kind of scale

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.000
and so become a bird?

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:13.000
And even that man himself might be descended from a group of tree-swinging apes?

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.000
In truth, the idea was not a new one.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:19.000
Other people before Darwin had suggested that all life on earth

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.000
might have a common ancestry.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.000
But Darwin went further.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:27.000
He gave the idea irresistible force by suggesting a mechanism

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.000
which might have brought that about.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.000
He called the mechanism natural selection.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.000
Put briefly, his argument was this.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:42.000
Individuals of the same species are not absolutely identical.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.000
Some of these giant tortoise hatchlings may have, from birth,

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.000
slightly longer necks than others.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.000
In times of drought, they will be able to reach leaves and live

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.000
while the shorter-necked ones die.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.000
So those best fitted for the environment will transmit their

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.000
characteristics to their offspring.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.000
After countless generations, the tortoises on arid islands

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.000
will have longer necks than those on well-watered ones.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.000
And so one species will have given rise to another.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:18.000
In these programs, we are going to survey the unmeasurable variety

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:22.000
of animals that have been produced by natural selection

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.000
and look at them not as isolated oddities,

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.000
but as elements in a long and continuing story

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.000
that began 1,000 million years ago

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.000
and is still continuing today.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.000
Some creatures, the mammals, such as these sea lions and myself,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.000
mammals both, are still living in the natural selection.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:44.000
The sea lions and myself, mammals both,

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.000
are relatively recent arrivals on the scene.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:51.000
Others, the birds, the reptiles, the amphibians, the fish,

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.000
have been here long, longer than we have.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.000
In places where conditions have remained virtually unchanged

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.000
over immense periods of time, there are still creatures living

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:05.000
which resemble very closely their early ancestors.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.000
They can tell us a lot.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.000
But in order to disentangle the story,

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.000
we also have to look for evidence in the rocks.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:25.000
The bodies of animals falling to the bottom of ancient seas and swamps

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:29.000
sometimes get entombed in the accumulating sediment.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:33.000
When, after millions of years, those sediments turn to rock,

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:40.000
the remains of animals and plants survive as fossils.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:44.960
The

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.960
The

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:55.000
The

00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:00.000
The

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.000
Since the discovery of radioactivity,

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:06.000
scientists have developed techniques of measuring the age of rocks

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.000
based on the rates at which some chemical elements decay.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:13.000
So fossils can be dated to within a few million years.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:15.000
But there are much simpler ways than that

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.000
of establishing the comparative ages of rocks that anyone can use.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:22.000
And there is no more dramatic place to do so

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:40.000
than in the Grand Canyon in the American West.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.000
The Colorado River, aided by wind and rain,

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:48.000
has cut a gigantic section through the sandstones and limestones of Arizona.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:51.000
The layers still lie largely undisturbed,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.000
so obviously the lower ones must have been deposited before the upper ones.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:59.000
So if we want to trace the ancestry of life back to its beginnings

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.000
in this part of the world,

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:16.000
we have to go deeper and deeper into the canyon.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:20.000
This is the greatest gash that exists in the surface of the Earth.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:24.000
From the rim to the river at the bottom is a vertical mile.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.000
There are a number of trails down,

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:51.000
and the usual way is to make the trip on the back of a mule.

00:12:51.000 --> 00:13:17.000
Here we're about 500 feet below the lip of the canyon,

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:21.000
and already the rocks are about 200 million years old.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.000
There are no mammal fossils to be found here,

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:27.000
but there are some four-legged land animals still,

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:30.000
small reptiles, a little lizard-like creature

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.000
that has left its tracks along here,

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:50.000
which was once the face of a sand dune.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:54.000
Farther down, there are no signs whatever of any reptiles,

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.000
but in limestones 400 million years old,

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:07.000
thousands of strange armored fish have been found.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:11.000
The trail winds on through rocks formed on the bottom of ancient seas.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:30.000
With every 20 feet we descend, we go back a further million years.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.000
The Grand Canyon is really two canyons, one inside the other.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:42.000
For a while, the trail flattens out as it approaches the rim of the inner canyon.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.000
And here I'm about two-thirds of the way down,

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:49.000
say about 3,500 feet below the rim,

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:54.000
and the rocks here are about 500 million years old.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:59.000
These rocks have no backbone animals in them at all, no fish.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:02.000
The only creatures there are are creatures without backbones,

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.000
including a whole lot of worms,

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:08.000
which have left this delicate tracery of trails

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:34.000
in what was mud on the bottom of a shallow sea.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:38.000
At last, the bottom and the Colorado River.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:42.000
It's taken nearly a day, going fairly easily, to get this far.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:45.000
We've ridden some seven miles of trail

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:52.000
and have descended that vertical mile into the Earth's crust.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:58.000
The rocks here are getting on for 2,000 million years old,

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:01.000
and for the past seven or eight hundred feet of our descent,

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:05.000
they've had no signs of any fossils at all in them.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:09.000
For many years, it was thought that all rocks of this great age

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:11.000
were without any fossils.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:15.000
Why was this? Was it because they were so unimaginably old

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:18.000
that they had had all traces of life crushed from them?

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:22.000
Or did life really begin with creatures as big as a worm?

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:25.000
For many years, this was a great puzzle.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.000
And then, about 20 or 30 years ago,

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:32.000
people realised that they had been looking in the wrong rocks

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.000
and in the wrong way.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.000
These are the Rite Rocks.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:46.000
They're a kind of flint or chert,

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:50.000
and they're on the shores of Lake Superior in Canada,

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:54.000
about 1,000 miles east and north of the Grand Canyon.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:57.000
They were well known during the last century

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:01.000
because the pioneers used them in their flintlock guns.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:05.000
And scientists have recognised, too, for a very long time,

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:08.000
that they were extremely ancient rocks.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.000
We now know that they are about the same age

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:14.000
as the rocks in the bottom of the Grand Canyon.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.000
That's to say, about 2,000 million years old.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.000
But these strange rings in them...

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:25.000
For a long time, these were a subject of great controversy.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:30.000
Some scientists maintained that they were a sign of very early life.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.000
Others, that they were no more than the result

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:38.000
of the ordinary chemical processes during the rocks' formation.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:40.000
But then, during the 1950s,

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:44.000
scientists started looking at them in the right way.

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:49.000
SIRENS WAIL

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:55.000
First of all, you have to cut a wafer-thin slice of the Gunflint rock.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:02.000
This is then ground down still further for several hours

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:05.000
until the slice is translucent.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:13.000
When scientists first prepared Gunflint chert

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:15.000
to look at through the microscope,

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:18.000
many people doubted that primitive forms of life,

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:21.000
even if they existed 2,000 million years ago,

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:24.000
could possibly be preserved as tiny fossils.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:30.000
And then scientists saw this.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.000
Marks in rocks can be deceptive.

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.000
They may just be the result of mineral action.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:40.000
But these filaments proved to be almost identical

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:43.000
to primitive algae growing today.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:49.000
The search continued.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:54.000
Soon the fossilised remains of many other kinds of primitive life

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:58.000
were found that had once lived in those early seas.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.000
And since those first discoveries,

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:08.000
other microfossils have been found elsewhere,

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.000
in rocks that are even more ancient,

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:13.000
some over 3,000 million years old.

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:19.000
These immense periods of time battle the imagination.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:21.000
But perhaps we can get some idea

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:24.000
of the relative lengths of the various stages

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:29.000
if we condense the whole history of life on Earth into one year.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.000
Then 10 million years become one day.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:35.000
And on that calendar,

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:39.000
I am talking in the very last moment of December 31st,

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:42.000
and primitive man will have appeared

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:45.000
only a few hours ago in the early afternoon.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:49.000
The first backboneed animal will have crawled up onto land

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.000
during the last week of November,

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:57.000
and these Gunflint Churchs will have been formed on June 15th.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:03.000
Now let's go back way, way to the beginning of January,

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:05.000
to the beginning of life.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:15.000
Over 3,500 million years ago,

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:18.000
our planet was radically different in almost every way

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:20.000
from the one we live on today.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:30.000
Erupting volcanoes built up islands of lava and ash in the global seas.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:34.000
The atmosphere was filled with gases such as ammonia, methane,

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:36.000
hydrogen and steam.

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:48.000
There was virtually no oxygen.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.000
In consequence, there was no ozone layer in the atmosphere.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:55.000
So ultraviolet rays in strengths that would be lethal to us

00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:00.000
bathed the young planet.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:29.000
The ultraviolet light, together with heat and electrical

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:31.000
and radioactive discharges,

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:34.000
brought about many chemical changes in the waters.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:38.000
Complex carbon compounds were formed, including amino acids,

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:40.000
the building blocks of protein.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:49.000
For millions of years, the chemical soup thickened and changed.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:55.000
Possibly some compounds were added to it from outer space.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:00.000
Some carbon compounds aggregated in droplets

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:05.000
with a membrane through which other chemicals could pass.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:16.000
Eventually, unusually large molecules appeared

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:19.000
which had extraordinary characteristics.

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:24.000
They caused amino acids to form around them and so built proteins.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:27.000
And they could also produce copies of themselves.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:33.000
Such a molecule, known as DNA, is at the centre of every life cell.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:39.000
Its shape is a double spiral linked by chemical units of just four kinds.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:43.000
Their arrangement acts as a code for the production of proteins

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:47.000
and a group of them in a section of DNA is called a gene.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:52.000
On occasion, the DNA unzips.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:55.000
Each half then attracts the correct chemical units

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:58.000
and so forms two new, identical molecules.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:02.000
When this first happened, primitive cells formed new cells

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:05.000
and life on Earth had appeared.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:12.000
But sometimes there is a mistake, a mutation.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:16.000
These caused variations in the first cells

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:19.000
and natural selection sorted them out.

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:25.000
Those that were as well or better suited to their environment survived.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:27.000
The rest died.

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:39.000
And so, over tens of millions of years,

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:43.000
a variety of bacteria-like organisms developed, thrived

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:46.000
and invaded new environments on Earth.

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:52.000
Evolution had truly begun.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:07.000
We can get a glimpse of what those first stirrings of life were like

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:12.000
in the hot volcanic springs of such places as Yellowstone Park in Wyoming.

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:24.000
And in these springs, staining them a whole variety of colours,

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:27.000
there flourish microorganisms.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:31.000
Microorganisms that look to be almost identical

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:34.000
with some of the earliest fossils that we know.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:38.000
Tufts of bacteria grow where the water is hottest.

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:47.000
In cooler areas, other bacteria deposit silica in strange coloured crusts.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:54.000
These bacteria represent the next big step,

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:57.000
for they are probably very like the first forms

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:00.000
to manufacture food inside their own cell walls,

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.000
with the help of energy from the sun, light.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:10.000
One of the raw materials they needed was hydrogen,

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:13.000
and at first they got it as sulphurated hydrogen,

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:16.000
which occurs conveniently enough in volcanic gases.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:20.000
There's some around here. This place smells a bit of rotten eggs.

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:26.000
And indeed, there's such bacteria flourishing in the hot water of these springs.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:30.000
But then that link with volcanoes was broken.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:34.000
Some forms of bacteria arose, which got their hydrogen

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:38.000
from a much more widespread and easily available source, from water.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:42.000
And that was a crucial stage in the history of life,

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:45.000
because if you take hydrogen from water,

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:49.000
you are left as a by-product with oxygen.

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:52.000
MUSIC

00:25:55.000 --> 00:26:00.000
These new blue-green bacteria, or cyanophytes, still exist,

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:05.000
as slime on wet rocks or in ponds covered with silver bubbles.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:11.000
It was they that first contributed oxygen in large quantities to the atmosphere.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:17.000
Under the microscope, you can see that they're very simple structures.

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:22.000
Some form chains, others are isolated beads.

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:33.000
On a larger scale, they form mats with bacteria

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:36.000
in the cooler springs of Yellowstone.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:45.000
Some of these blue-greens deposit lime

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:48.000
as part of the chemistry of their body processes.

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:54.000
And in one place in the world, here, in a bay on the coast of Western Australia,

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:59.000
they grow large and huge to form these great pillars.

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:03.000
What makes this place so special is that the mouth of the bay

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:08.000
is almost blocked by a bar of sand covered with seagrass.

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:11.000
And this restricts the flow of the tide in and out,

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:15.000
with the result that these waters are extremely salty.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:21.000
And so virtually none of the creatures which eat blue-greens can survive here.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:25.000
And so these blue-greens, these very primitive organisms,

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:31.000
can grow uncropped, just as they did when they were the most advanced form of life

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:36.000
2,000 million years ago, at the beginning of life on Earth.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:41.000
And here is an explanation for those extraordinary shapes

00:27:41.000 --> 00:28:07.000
that we saw on the shores of Lake Superior.

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:15.000
This is as close as we may ever get to a scene of the world

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:19.000
when life was at last beginning to stir.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:27.000
Now life had reached a point of no return.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:31.000
The oxygen accumulated and formed a layer of ozone in the atmosphere

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:34.000
screening off ultraviolet rays, the very source of energy

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:37.000
that had helped to create the first life,

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:41.000
so it could never begin in the same way again.

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:51.000
Outwardly, things changed little for hundreds of millions of years.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:57.000
But eventually the state was set for a new and dramatic step.

00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:22.000
To find evidence of that development, you need go no further than your local pond.

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:26.000
Most microscopic organisms here are just single cells,

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:32.000
yet each is very much more complex than any bacterium.

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:37.000
Some, like this amoeba, seem to have animal characteristics,

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:41.000
and some appear to be simple plants.

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:45.000
Yet others seem to be half animal and half plant.

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:49.000
In terms of complexity, they are about as different from a bacterium

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:52.000
as a man is from a jellyfish.

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:58.000
To see why, we have to look inside one of them with an electron microscope.

00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:03.000
The DNA, unlike that in the bacterium, is enclosed in its own compartment.

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:10.000
Other parts of the cell resemble and act like blue greens.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:17.000
These look more like bacteria and are a source of energy.

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:24.000
This particular cell is driven by a tail that resembles yet another type of bacterium.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:30.000
So it appears that this tiny creature is composed of a committee of smaller ones.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:34.000
And indeed, many people now believe that it was by some form of collaboration

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:42.000
between primitive cells that such organisms came into existence.

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:46.000
But it took a long time for life to reach this stage.

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:50.000
Probably not until some 1200 million years ago,

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:58.000
say early September in our life on Earth year.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:02.000
These plant cells belong to this new advanced type.

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:06.000
Many kinds of them still abound in freshwater and the sea,

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:16.000
and they form the basic food of other simple organisms.

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:44.000
Some of them have delicate skeletons of silica.

00:31:56.000 --> 00:32:00.000
This is another kind, with chambered shell of chalk,

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:07.000
and so small that several would fit on the head of a pin.

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:13.000
The animals, in essence, are like an amoeba to which they're closely related.

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:19.000
They catch their food with sticky threads.

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:21.000
When something tangles with them,

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:30.000
it's drawn inside and digested in a special compartment.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:38.000
The cells can reproduce by splitting into two, as bacteria do.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:44.000
But some cells have more complicated methods of reproduction.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:48.000
These have temporarily joined so that they can exchange genes.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:58.000
Later, they will part and then divide in the normal way.

00:32:58.000 --> 00:33:03.000
In other cases, cells shuffle their genes and then divide in such a way

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:06.000
that they produce a very special kind of cell

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:09.000
with only half the number of genes of the parent.

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:15.000
These special cells are eggs.

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:21.000
Meanwhile, other members of the same species are also producing sex cells

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:25.000
with half rations of shuffled genes.

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:28.000
This time, they're quite different in form.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:34.000
They have tails. They're sperm cells.

00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:40.000
They're chemically attracted to the egg,

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:48.000
and the first one to find it down there on the bottom penetrates the wall.

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:52.000
After getting inside, it swims towards the nucleus and unites with it

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:55.000
so that the full complement of genes is restored.

00:33:55.000 --> 00:34:02.000
But now it's in a new combination, different from either parent.

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:04.000
When this mechanism developed,

00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:10.000
the extent and frequency of variation greatly increased.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:16.000
As a result, the pace of evolution accelerated.

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:23.000
One of the most successful groups of single-celled creatures

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:26.000
in this microscopic world are the ciliates.

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:29.000
They're covered with a coat of beating hairs, the cilia,

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:31.000
which drive them through the water.

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:35.000
The cilia also create currents which waft tiny particles of food

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:39.000
into their gullets.

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:43.000
These particular ciliates are stalked and remain anchored to one spot.

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:48.000
But others are large and mobile and actively hunt for their food.

00:34:48.000 --> 00:35:00.620
They're also very active in the

00:35:00.620 --> 00:35:24.620
These ciliates are among the larger single-celled creatures,

00:35:24.620 --> 00:35:27.620
just visible to the naked eye.

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:31.620
Above this size, it seems that the chemical processes inside

00:35:31.620 --> 00:35:33.620
become difficult and inefficient.

00:35:33.620 --> 00:35:36.620
But size can be achieved in a different way,

00:35:36.620 --> 00:35:43.620
by grouping cells together in an organized colony.

00:35:43.620 --> 00:35:46.620
This volvox, almost the size of a pinhead,

00:35:46.620 --> 00:35:50.620
is composed of hundreds of cells, each with a tiny tail,

00:35:50.620 --> 00:35:58.620
beating in a coordinated way.

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:01.620
Inside, daughter colonies are formed,

00:36:01.620 --> 00:36:09.620
and the tiny, delicate globe ruptures to release them.

00:36:09.620 --> 00:36:15.620
Eventually, this coordination between cells was taken a stage further.

00:36:15.620 --> 00:36:23.620
Sponges appeared.

00:36:23.620 --> 00:36:27.620
There are about 5,000 species of sponges in existence today,

00:36:27.620 --> 00:36:31.620
and in all of them, the colonial bonds between their constituent cells

00:36:31.620 --> 00:36:33.620
are remarkably loose.

00:36:33.620 --> 00:36:37.620
Individual cells may crawl around over the surface like a mebee.

00:36:37.620 --> 00:36:40.620
If a sponge is forced through a fine sieve,

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:43.620
so that it's completely broken down into separate cells,

00:36:43.620 --> 00:36:48.620
they will, if left alone, reorganize themselves to form a new sponge.

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:54.620
And what is more, each kind of cell will take up its proper place.

00:36:54.620 --> 00:36:57.620
Some are specialized to form the walls.

00:36:57.620 --> 00:37:00.620
Others are pump cells that line the walls of the channels

00:37:00.620 --> 00:37:03.620
with which the sponge is riddled.

00:37:03.620 --> 00:37:06.620
By beating their tiny threads, they create currents,

00:37:06.620 --> 00:37:09.620
drawing in the water through the pores on the sides,

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:16.620
and then pumping it out at the top after the food has been strained off.

00:37:16.620 --> 00:37:21.620
The structure is supported by yet other cells which make tiny needles,

00:37:21.620 --> 00:37:23.620
and these build to form a skeleton.

00:37:23.620 --> 00:37:31.620
In the so-called glass sponges, they're made of silica.

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:35.620
Modern science is only some 200 or 300 years old,

00:37:35.620 --> 00:37:39.620
but already it's provided us with some profound insights

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:41.620
into the workings of the world around us.

00:37:41.620 --> 00:37:44.620
But there's still a very great deal that we don't know.

00:37:44.620 --> 00:37:48.620
Take this sponge skeleton, for example.

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:52.620
How on earth did the microscopic sponge cells,

00:37:52.620 --> 00:37:56.620
one of the most primitive organisms that we know,

00:37:56.620 --> 00:38:01.620
collaborate to build out of a million splinters of silica

00:38:01.620 --> 00:38:08.620
this complex and beautiful structure which is sometimes called Venus' flower basket?

00:38:08.620 --> 00:38:11.620
Some religious people will maintain that it is the work of God,

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:14.620
and that is all that need be said.

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:17.620
Some scientists will claim that it's only a matter of time

00:38:17.620 --> 00:38:21.620
before we will provide a very much more detailed explanation than that.

00:38:21.620 --> 00:38:26.620
Either way, it remains an awesome and beautiful object.

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:30.620
But sponges, in an evolutionary sense, are a dead end.

00:38:30.620 --> 00:38:35.620
They have no two mouth, no gut, no muscles, no nervous system.

00:38:35.620 --> 00:38:38.620
But this has.

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:46.620
It's a jelly-like creature with just two layers of cells.

00:38:46.620 --> 00:38:50.620
The inner one lines a cavity which has a single opening.

00:38:50.620 --> 00:38:52.620
Its design may be simple,

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:56.620
but nevertheless it is a fully coordinated, multi-celled animal.

00:38:56.620 --> 00:38:59.620
It's one of several kinds of comb jellies

00:38:59.620 --> 00:39:03.620
which swarm in the oceans but which are so transparent

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:06.620
that they're hardly ever noticed.

00:39:14.620 --> 00:39:17.620
To appreciate the full beauty of comb jellies,

00:39:17.620 --> 00:39:20.620
you have to use special lighting.

00:39:20.620 --> 00:39:24.620
They swim with rows of cilia arranged like combs,

00:39:24.620 --> 00:39:51.620
and their beating produces interference colours like a rainbow.

00:39:54.620 --> 00:40:16.620
This pulsating bell is a close relation of the comb jelly.

00:40:16.620 --> 00:40:18.620
Technically it's called a medusa,

00:40:18.620 --> 00:40:21.620
after the unfortunate lady in the Greek myth

00:40:21.620 --> 00:40:23.620
who had snakes on her head for hair.

00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:27.620
Its tentacles have stings for capturing prey.

00:40:27.620 --> 00:40:31.620
Once caught, it's transferred to the mouth at the centre.

00:40:37.620 --> 00:40:40.620
Comb jellies and medusa both have muscle fibres

00:40:40.620 --> 00:40:43.620
and a simple nervous system.

00:40:43.620 --> 00:40:45.620
But most medusa have a surprise.

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:49.620
They begin their lives in a completely different form,

00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:53.620
like this.

00:40:53.620 --> 00:40:56.620
These may look like plants, but they're animals.

00:40:56.620 --> 00:40:59.620
Each structure began when a tiny free-swimming creature

00:40:59.620 --> 00:41:02.620
developed from the fertilised egg of a medusa

00:41:02.620 --> 00:41:05.620
and settled on the bottom of the sea or a piece of weed.

00:41:05.620 --> 00:41:08.620
From it sprouted a tiny branching twig

00:41:08.620 --> 00:41:11.620
bearing flower-like individuals called polyps.

00:41:11.620 --> 00:41:15.620
These filter feed with the aid of beating cilia and grow,

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:21.620
putting out more branches with polyps on them.

00:41:21.620 --> 00:41:25.620
Each polyp is basically equivalent to a swimming medusa.

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:28.620
In some species, medusa can bud directly off the branch

00:41:28.620 --> 00:41:30.620
and swim away.

00:41:30.620 --> 00:41:46.620
In others, they are born from special vessels.

00:42:00.620 --> 00:42:03.620
All these medusa, which are not much bigger than a pinhead,

00:42:03.620 --> 00:42:06.620
have been produced by a process that involves no sex.

00:42:06.620 --> 00:42:09.620
Eventually, they themselves develop

00:42:09.620 --> 00:42:28.660
and are then fed to the

00:42:28.660 --> 00:42:38.660
new colonies of polyps again.

00:42:38.660 --> 00:42:41.660
This alternation of generations between sexual and non-sexual

00:42:41.660 --> 00:42:44.660
methods of reproduction has given these creatures

00:42:44.660 --> 00:42:48.660
and their relatives great scope for variety.

00:42:48.660 --> 00:43:10.660
The larger medusa carry quantities of jelly in their umbrellas

00:43:10.660 --> 00:43:13.660
to make them more robust in rough seas.

00:43:13.660 --> 00:43:16.660
These are the true jellyfish, and many of them lead

00:43:16.660 --> 00:43:19.660
the same type of double life, having a stationary polyp phase

00:43:19.660 --> 00:43:42.660
as well as a swimming one.

00:43:42.660 --> 00:43:46.660
There's a surprising variety of types of jellyfish.

00:43:46.660 --> 00:43:49.660
Some are able to feed on quite large prey,

00:43:49.660 --> 00:43:52.660
but this one has elaborate ruffles in which there are many pores

00:43:52.660 --> 00:44:11.660
for netting microscopic food.

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:14.660
This shallow water species uses pulsating movements

00:44:14.660 --> 00:44:30.660
to create currents of water that bring it food.

00:44:30.660 --> 00:44:33.660
It's an obvious deduction that such simple things as jellyfish

00:44:33.660 --> 00:44:36.660
appeared very early in the development of life,

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:39.660
but for a long time there was no actual proof

00:44:39.660 --> 00:44:42.660
that they did.

00:44:42.660 --> 00:44:45.660
After all, proof could only come from the fossil record,

00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:48.660
and who could suppose that such an insubstantial thing

00:44:48.660 --> 00:44:51.660
as a jellyfish could be fossilized,

00:44:51.660 --> 00:44:54.660
let alone survive in rocks from the earliest period?

00:44:54.660 --> 00:44:57.660
And then about 30 years ago, in these sandstones

00:44:57.660 --> 00:45:00.660
in the Flinders Ranges in southern Australia,

00:45:00.660 --> 00:45:04.660
which are probably about 650 million years old,

00:45:04.660 --> 00:45:13.660
people found things like this.

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:16.660
At first, many scientists refused to believe

00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:20.660
that these faint impressions were the remains of jellyfish,

00:45:20.660 --> 00:45:23.660
but by now enough specimens have been discovered

00:45:23.660 --> 00:45:28.660
to make quite sure that that indeed is what they are.

00:45:28.660 --> 00:45:31.660
What's more, almost a dozen different species

00:45:31.660 --> 00:45:42.660
have now been discovered.

00:45:42.660 --> 00:45:45.660
Such fossils as these reveal that at a very early period

00:45:45.660 --> 00:45:48.660
jellyfish existed in many different forms,

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:55.660
just as they do today.

00:45:55.660 --> 00:45:58.660
This, though a close relative of the jellyfish,

00:45:58.660 --> 00:46:01.660
is strictly speaking not a single creature,

00:46:01.660 --> 00:46:04.660
but a colony of polyps, one that has gone to sea

00:46:04.660 --> 00:46:12.660
and assumed much the same structure as a true jellyfish.

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:15.660
Another colony that is built on the same principle

00:46:15.660 --> 00:46:18.660
is known as the Portuguese Manawar.

00:46:18.660 --> 00:46:21.660
It has no swimming bell, but instead a bag filled with gas

00:46:21.660 --> 00:46:25.660
that supports the whole colony.

00:46:25.660 --> 00:46:28.660
While the air is annoyed drying out and overheating,

00:46:28.660 --> 00:46:31.660
the colony is able to dip the sail into the water

00:46:31.660 --> 00:46:37.660
from time to time.

00:46:37.660 --> 00:46:47.660
Long tentacles trail behind for lengths of up to 50 metres.

00:46:47.660 --> 00:46:50.660
The colony begins with just one founding member,

00:46:50.660 --> 00:46:53.660
which buds off two lines of other individuals.

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:56.660
Some of them bud off others, some specialise for feeding,

00:46:56.660 --> 00:47:01.660
some for reproduction, and some to catch prey.

00:47:01.660 --> 00:47:04.660
As with all jellyfish and their relatives,

00:47:04.660 --> 00:47:07.660
the tentacles have special stinging cells.

00:47:07.660 --> 00:47:10.660
Each contains a coiled barbed tube which discharges

00:47:10.660 --> 00:47:16.660
immediately on contact with its prey.

00:47:16.660 --> 00:47:21.660
And from the end of each comes a drop of paralysing poison.

00:47:21.660 --> 00:47:24.660
Fossils like the Portuguese Manawar are highly complicated creatures,

00:47:24.660 --> 00:47:27.660
and you might think that they are comparatively recent developments

00:47:27.660 --> 00:47:29.660
in the world of jellyfish.

00:47:29.660 --> 00:47:32.660
In fact, one of the fossils from the Flinders range

00:47:32.660 --> 00:47:41.660
suggests that such colonies existed 650 million years ago.

00:47:41.660 --> 00:47:44.660
The impression in this rock is thought to be from a gas bag

00:47:44.660 --> 00:47:48.660
of such a colony of polyps, which was blown inshore

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:52.660
and cast up on the sandy beaches that today form the sandstones

00:47:52.660 --> 00:48:01.660
of the Flinders ranges.

00:48:01.660 --> 00:48:03.660
And that's not all.

00:48:03.660 --> 00:48:06.660
Alongside those jellyfish in the same rocks,

00:48:06.660 --> 00:48:10.660
there are the remains of other closely related creatures.

00:48:10.660 --> 00:48:15.660
These beautiful encrashments are of animals in which the equivalent

00:48:15.660 --> 00:48:19.660
of the Medusa remained very small and attached to one another

00:48:19.660 --> 00:48:21.660
to form a colony.

00:48:21.660 --> 00:48:25.660
And we can be pretty sure that this is what that was,

00:48:25.660 --> 00:48:28.660
because very similar creatures are alive today

00:48:28.660 --> 00:48:35.660
and living once more only about 40 miles away from here in the sea.

00:48:35.660 --> 00:48:37.660
These are sea pens.

00:48:37.660 --> 00:48:40.660
On either side of the stem are polyps which are specialized

00:48:40.660 --> 00:48:43.660
for feeding and reproduction.

00:48:43.660 --> 00:48:56.660
This living one bears a remarkable resemblance to the fossil.

00:48:56.660 --> 00:49:00.660
They were given the name sea pen when people wrote with quills,

00:49:00.660 --> 00:49:08.660
and apt it must have seemed, for the skeleton is flexible and horny.

00:49:08.660 --> 00:49:12.660
They belong to a group called the soft corals.

00:49:12.660 --> 00:49:16.660
This is another kind, a soft flabby organism rather ghoulishly known

00:49:16.660 --> 00:49:24.660
as dead man's fingers.

00:49:24.660 --> 00:49:30.660
Soft corals of one kind or another can grow in depths of up to 6,000 meters,

00:49:30.660 --> 00:49:33.660
but stony corals, the ones which produce limestone skeletons

00:49:33.660 --> 00:49:41.660
and form reefs, can live no deeper than 40 meters.

00:49:41.660 --> 00:49:45.660
The coral polyps live only on the surface of these structures,

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:49.660
each in its tiny limestone cell and connected to its neighbors

00:49:49.660 --> 00:49:54.660
by thin strands so that the whole skin is a living network.

00:49:54.660 --> 00:49:57.660
As new ones sprout from the connecting branches,

00:49:57.660 --> 00:50:01.660
they secrete cells for themselves which grow over the early ones

00:50:01.660 --> 00:50:06.660
and stifle them.

00:50:06.660 --> 00:50:09.660
The coral tissues contain plants,

00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:12.660
tiny single-celled green algae.

00:50:12.660 --> 00:50:18.660
Like all plants, they release oxygen, which helps the coral polyps to respire.

00:50:18.660 --> 00:50:22.660
They also assimilate carbon dioxide, taking it from the water,

00:50:22.660 --> 00:50:35.660
and that helps the corals to form their gigantic skeletons of lime.

00:50:35.660 --> 00:50:38.660
Each species branches and buds in a different way,

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:42.660
and so the colony produces its own individual shape.

00:51:08.660 --> 00:51:31.660
The reef may look like some fantastic multicolored jungle of plants and flowers,

00:51:31.660 --> 00:51:38.660
but when you touch one, it has the hard, incongruous scratch of stone.

00:52:02.660 --> 00:52:07.660
The coral organisms are tiny and simple,

00:52:07.660 --> 00:52:12.660
and yet they grow on such a scale and their stony skeletons are so durable

00:52:12.660 --> 00:52:15.660
that they may well have been the first signs of life

00:52:15.660 --> 00:52:18.660
that could be detected from outer space.

00:52:18.660 --> 00:52:22.660
Certainly, this Great Barrier Reef can be seen from the Moon.

00:52:22.660 --> 00:52:29.660
So it may well be that if a passing astronaut came this way several hundred million years ago,

00:52:29.660 --> 00:52:33.660
he might have noticed in the deep blue seas of the Earth

00:52:33.660 --> 00:52:38.660
a few mysterious beautiful shapes in turquoise

00:52:38.660 --> 00:53:00.660
and guessed that life on Earth had really started.

00:54:08.660 --> 00:54:11.660
